# XCU 2.2-quoting
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.1
class      required
says       An unquoted backslash makes the next character stand for itself:
           each of | & ; < > ( ) $ backquote backslash double-quote
           single-quote space * ? [ # ~ written after a backslash reaches
           the command as that character.
test       t/001-backslash-literal.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.1
class      required
says       A backslash followed by a newline is removed entirely before the
           input is split into tokens, so it joins the text on both sides
           and does not separate tokens; a blank written after the removed
           pair still does.
test       t/002-line-continuation.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.2
class      required
says       Characters inside single quotes keep their literal value,
           including every character that is special elsewhere and a
           newline; a single quote can be added to a word only outside
           single quotes.
test       t/003-single-quotes.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       Inside double quotes the characters other than dollar sign,
           backquote and backslash keep their literal value: operators, glob
           characters, a single quote, a hash, a tilde, white space and
           newlines are not special, and no filename expansion happens.
test       t/004-double-quotes-literal.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       Inside double quotes the dollar sign still introduces parameter
           expansion, command substitution and arithmetic expansion, and the
           backquote still introduces the old form of command substitution.
test       t/005-double-quotes-dollar.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       Inside double quotes a backslash is an escape only before a
           dollar sign, backquote, backslash, double quote or newline;
           before any other character both the backslash and that character
           stay.
test       t/006-double-quotes-backslash.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
since      2024
says       Inside double quotes a dollar sign followed by a single quote
           does not start dollar-single-quotes: the dollar sign, the quotes
           and the text between them are all literal.
test       t/007-double-quotes-dollar-single.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       The commands inside a $( ) that sits within double quotes are not
           affected by those double quotes: they can hold their own quotes,
           and a quoted parenthesis does not end the substitution.
test       t/008-double-quotes-cmdsub-nesting.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       When double quotes enclose a parameter expansion or a command
           substitution, every character of the result keeps its literal
           value: no field splitting, no filename expansion and no further
           expansion.
test       t/009-double-quotes-expansion-result.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.010
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       Inside double quotes, in the text of a ${ } expansion such as
           ${u:-word}, characters other than double quote, backquote, dollar
           sign and backslash keep their literal value (a single quote is
           literal), a backslash before a closing brace is an escape that
           hides it from the search for the end, and a nested $( ) has its
           own quoting.
test       t/010-double-quotes-braces.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.011
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.6.2
class      required
says       For the four substring forms (# ## % %%) the enclosing double
           quotes have no effect on special characters inside the braces:
           pattern characters stay active, while quotes or a backslash
           inside the braces make a character literal.
test       t/011-double-quotes-substring-forms.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.012
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.4
class      required
since      2024
says       Inside dollar-single-quotes the sequences backslash-double-quote,
           backslash-single-quote, double backslash, and backslash followed
           by a, b, e, f, n, r, t or v yield the double quote, single quote,
           backslash, alert, backspace, escape, form-feed, newline,
           carriage-return, tab and vertical-tab characters.
test       t/012-dollar-single-escapes.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.013
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.4
class      required
since      2024
says       In dollar-single-quotes, backslash-c followed by a letter yields
           the matching control character (A gives value 1, Z gives value
           26), and backslash-c followed by two backslashes yields the FS
           control character (value 28).
test       t/013-dollar-single-control.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.014
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.4
class      required
since      2024
says       In dollar-single-quotes, backslash-x followed by hexadecimal
           digits yields the byte with that value, and the digits end at the
           first character that is not a hexadecimal digit (tested with one
           and two digits).
test       t/014-dollar-single-hex.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.015
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.4
class      required
since      2024
says       In dollar-single-quotes, a backslash followed by one to three
           octal digits yields the byte with that value; the sequence ends
           after three digits or at the first character that is not an octal
           digit.
test       t/015-dollar-single-octal.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.016
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.4
class      required
since      2024
says       Other characters inside dollar-single-quotes keep their literal
           value, so dollar signs, backquotes, parentheses and glob
           characters are not expanded, and the result is a single word that
           is not split.
test       t/016-dollar-single-literal.sh

assertion  XCU.2.2-quoting.017
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.4
class      required
since      2024
says       In dollar-single-quotes an escaped single quote does not end the
           quoted text, and the quoted text can be joined with neighbouring
           quoted or unquoted text into one word.
test       t/017-dollar-single-quote-end.sh
